  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 215 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nSaturday through Tuesday:\n\nSynopsis:\nWarm weather continues into Saturday (highs in the low 60s) with\na south breeze. A period of rain showers is expected (60 to 70\npercent chances) at some point between Saturday afternoon and\nSunday morning ahead of and along a passing cold front; exact\ntiming depends on when the front passes through. A sharp cooling\ntrend then kicks in behind the front, plunging daytime highs to\nthe low 40s next Monday and Tuesday. Lows in the 20s are expected\nfor inland areas early next week.\n\nDiscussion:\nSaturday morning features a high-amplitude trough over the \nRocky Mountains and low pressure tracking northeastward into \nOntario Canada. Surface high pressure (the same one from earlier\nthis week) manages to linger over the northeastern CONUS \nstates, with a tight pressure gradient over WI driving a south \nbreeze. Continued WAA allows daytime highs to remain in the low \n60s Saturday, despite approaching cloud cover.\n\nThe greatest differences we're seeing between ECMWF and GFS \nmodel guidance this weekend is the timing of the cold front (and\nassociated precip ahead of and along it). Rain could arrive as \nearly as Saturday afternoon or hold off until Sunday morning. \nThe latest model trends have been to delay the arrival of the \nfront slightly, hence precip chances for Saturday afternoon are \nfalling and Sunday morning is on the rise.\n\nWinds veer NW and become breezy again behind the front (either \nSunday morning or afternoon). Exact front timing will determine \nSunday's high temperatures, for now we're going with upper 40s \nas a 'middle ground' solution. A delayed front could trend that \nwarmer. Highs temps then hit the low 40s (perhaps even 40 \ndegrees flat) for Monday and Tuesday. Lows will be in the 20s \nearly next week, perhaps locally warmer by Lake MI, due to some\nweak onshore flow. Likely dry weather Monday and Tuesday as \nhigh pressure settles into the Great Lakes region. \n\nSheppard",
